      "sentiment": null
    },
    {
      "title": "DepEd: Devolution, not decentralization",
      "desc": "In December of 2023, Pulse Asia Research released the results of a survey on the proposal to decentralize…",
      "content": "DepEd: Devolution, not decentralization - BusinessWorld Online n December of 2023, Pulse Asia Research released the results of a survey on the proposal to decentralize education. More specifically, the question asked in Pilipino was: There are studies currently being conducted on the decentralization of education. This means that the local government units will run all public schools. Do you agree or disagree with the proposal? As shown in the accompanying table, 64% agreed that the local government units run all public schools, 13% disagreed, while 23% had no opinion. Noteworthy is the response from the Visayas Region where 81% agreed while only 3% disagreed with 15% having no opinion. Moreover, if we consider only those who had an opinion or knowledge of the issue, 83% of those who had an opinion agreed while 17% disagreed. For the Visayas Region, 96% of those who had an opinion agreed while only 4% disagreed. rm indication of the extreme dissatisfaction of parents and the general public with the ways the Department of Education (DepEd) has been running the public schools. To clarify, what is being proposed is devolution, not decentralization. Devolution is the transfer of power, authority, functions, or resources from the National Government to LGUs. Decentralization is the transfer of powers or delegation of authority from the DepEd Central Office to Regional Offices, Schools Division Offices Schools, and Learning Centers, collectively referred to as “field offices.” The Local Government Code of 1991 devolved responsibility for the delivery of basic services in the health, agriculture, social welfare, public works, and environment sectors from the national agencies to LGUs. Basic education was not devolved to the LGUs, contrary to the policy in most countries like Vietnam of devolving basic education to local government units. Instead, EdCom I (the first Congressional Commission on Education) in its 1991 Report recommended the decentralization of basic education. Ten years later, decentralization has not been achieved. In response to this failure to decentralize, Congress passed Republic Act (RA) 9155 or Governance of Basic Education Act mandating the decentralization of the Department of Education. Twenty-five years later, decentralization has not been achieved. In response, in 2025 EdCom II commissioned a study to review the decentralization experience since the passage of that law. Entitled, “Realizing Shared Governance Decentralization of Philippine Basic Education” and authored by Reynaldo Laguda, Gil Aquino, Linda Cangayao, and Ria Tagle, the study aimed to analyze the barriers to decentralizing basic education governance in the Philippines to ensure future reforms are effective and sustainable. The key concept behind decentralization is School Based Management (SBM). School Based Management is a strategy to improve education by transferring significant decision-making authority from the Central Office to the individual schools. This was specifically mandated by RA 9155: “the school shall be the heart of the formal education system” and “the seat of learning.” Schools “shall be empowered to make decisions on what is the best for the learners they serve.” The parents and the community shall also be encouraged to be actively involved in education. RA No. 10533 further provides that the curriculum should be flexible enough for schools to localize, indigenize, and enhance it based on their respective educational and social contexts. Unfortunately, while this strategy was adopted by several DepEd Secretaries notably Brother Armin Luistro, decentralization as reported in the study was never implemented for several reasons. 1.) The key official in School Based Management is the School Head or Principal. Under RA 9155, the role of the principal was defined as: Oversees the entire school or cluster of schools. Sets the mission, vision, goals, and objectives of the school. Responsible for creating a conducive environment for t",
