      "title": "Scientists Uncover 6,000-Year-Old Human Remains. No Known Living Population Share Their DNA",
      "desc": "Seven people lived 6,000 years ago. Their DNA points to a lineage that seems to vanish from history.",
      "content": "Scientists Uncover 6,000-Year-Old Human Remains. No Known Living Population Share Their DNA Seven people who lived about 6,000 years ago were excavated at , an archaeological site roughly 3,000 meters (about 9,800 feet) up on the high plains north of Bogotá. They were from a comparatively small population, and their bones and teeth still held DNA. ever published from Colombia, said Cosimo Posth, the paleogeneticist who was the study’s senior author. Then the team went looking for the seven’s descendants and found none. The lineage is missing from people who lived on the same plains 2,000 years ago. It is missing from every later group the team sampled there, and the researchers report that it contributed nothing to “We couldn’t find descendants of these early hunter-gatherers of the Colombian high plains,” said Kim-Louise Krettek, the study’s first author, in . The genes, she said, “were not passed on.” South America was settled from the north, and the first hunter-gatherers entered through what is now Colombia. Andrea Casas-Vargas, a geneticist at the Universidad Nacional de Colombia and a co-author, the region is “the gateway to the South American continent.” The Checua people were not a separate branch of humanity. Krettek said they “derive from the earliest population that spread and differentiated across South America very rapidly.” What sets them apart is everything that did not follow. The authors said that the group shows no particular affinity to and made no genetic contribution to ancient or present-day South Americans. That caught outside researchers off guard. “This is unexpected,” Andre Luiz Campelo dos Santos, a Florida Atlantic University archaeologist who was not part of the study, told the AP. “Up to this point, we didn’t believe there was any other lineage that would appear in South America.” Until this study, Colombia had been “a blank spot in ancient DNA studies of the Americas,” Harvard University anthropologist Christina Warinner, who was also not involved, The People Who Came Next Carried Central American Ancestry , the high plains around Bogotá. Together they cover almost 6,000 years, ending shortly before Spanish colonization. After the seven from Checua, the next group is nine people from the , about 2,000 years ago. Their ancestry is predominantly related to populations from . So is the ancestry of the remaining five: three people associated with the , who lived between 1,200 and 520 years ago, and two from the Los Curos area, about 530 years ago. The newcomers brought more than genes. Casas-Vargas said they probably carried ceramics and the into what is now Colombia. Branches of that language family are still spoken in Central America. A turnover this complete is rare on the continent. “That genetic traces of the original population disappear completely is unusual, especially in South America,” Casas-Vargas said. In the Andes and the southern cone, researchers have found strong over long periods and through cultural change. The Altiplano itself shows that pattern once the Central American ancestry arrives. It holds from the Herrera period through the rise of the Muisca until Europeans arrived about 500 years ago, even though, as Casas-Vargas put it, “there was massive cultural change.” Before finishing the study, the team met with the Guardia Indígena Muisca, living descendants of the Muisca culture on the high plains. “The genetic disposition must not be viewed as equal to cultural identity,” Posth said. Four Thousand Years the DNA Does Not Cover Nobody knows what happened to the Checua lineage. Casas-Vargas told CNN the findings raise questions “as to where they came from and why they disappeared.” The AP reported that scientists are not sure whether the group was absorbed into larger populations or pushed out. listed the possibilities archaeologists have raised: environmental change, replacement by other groups, or mixing with neighbors until the distinctive DNA was diluted away. The genomes cann",
